<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide build policy, imported automatically into every project under the repository root.

    Before this file existed, each of these properties was copy-pasted into all 17 project files.
    Nothing compared the copies, so they drifted silently: the same divergence is what let six
    package versions fork across the test suites (see Directory.Packages.props).

    A project that genuinely needs a different value overrides it locally — project files are
    evaluated after this one, so a local setting still wins. Overrides are therefore visible as
    deliberate exceptions rather than invisible as copies. No project overrides Nullable today:
    every one of them compiles with nullable reference types enabled.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Roslyn code analysis. The built-in .NET analyzers are on by default for net10.0; these two properties
    are the part the SDK does not decide for us.

    AnalysisLevel is pinned rather than left at its 'latest' default. Left floating, every .NET SDK upgrade
    can enable rules nobody opted into, and because warnings are errors here that lands as a red build
    rather than as advice. This is the same reason package versions are pinned centrally: a version — or a
    rule set — that is stated once cannot drift. Raise it deliberately, with the new rules' findings fixed
    in the same change.

    AnalysisModeSecurity turns the whole security category on. Measured before enabling: one finding in the
    entire repository, so the category costs nothing to hold at maximum and would otherwise report a real
    XML-parsing weakness only as an IDE suggestion. The other categories stay at Default and the rules
    worth having from them are opted into individually in .globalconfig, because the two largest rules a
    category mode would bring in (CA1707 on test names, CA2007 on ConfigureAwait) do not apply repo-wide — and
    CA2007 demands the opposite of what the GUI's architecture rules forbid (ADR-001). CA2007 is enabled for
    the SDK alone, in ihcclient/analyser.config.

    AnalysisMode is raised here for every project the repository ships or runs — the SDK, the two Avalonia
    applications, the shared bootstrap libraries, the examples and the utilities. Recommended is the middle of
    the ladder (None < Default < Minimum < Recommended < All) and a strict superset of Minimum; it is the level
    at which a latent culture, allocation, disposal or contract defect is reported at build time rather than
    found in a run. The rules a project declines out of that mode are turned off one at a time, each with its
    reason, in .globalconfig for the decisions that hold repo-wide and in ihcclient/analyser.config for the ones
    that are the SDK's alone — which also names the one rule scoped by path outside it.

    The TEST suites stay at Default, in tests/Directory.Build.props. That is not an exemption on principle: what
    the mode adds over Default is performance and API-shape rules aimed at code that ships, and a test assembly
    ships nowhere. The correctness rules opted into in .globalconfig apply to the test suites unchanged.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisModeSecurity>All</AnalysisModeSecurity>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
